# `npm install` fails outright without this, and the reason is not guessable.
#
# @playwright/test is pinned to EXACTLY 1.48.0 (see the note in
# playwright.config.ts): Playwright 1.5x and later ship no Chromium or WebKit
# build for macOS 12, which is what this project is developed on, so a newer
# version installs and then cannot install a browser.
#
# next@16 declares `peerOptional @playwright/test: ^1.51.1`. npm 7+ treats an
# unsatisfiable optional peer as a hard ERESOLVE failure, so the pin and the
# peer range cannot coexist under the default resolver — a fresh clone gets
# "could not resolve" and no node_modules at all.
#
# The peer is OPTIONAL and only describes Next's own `next experimental-test`
# integration, which this project does not use. Nothing in the app imports
# Playwright; it is the end-to-end runner and nothing else. So the correct
# resolution really is "ignore that peer", and this makes that the committed,
# reproducible behaviour instead of something every contributor has to
# rediscover by passing --legacy-peer-deps by hand.
#
# If the macOS 12 constraint ever goes away, the better fix is to unpin
# Playwright and delete this file — not to keep both.
legacy-peer-deps=true
